00:04:56.389 --> 00:05:01.770
So, we've thrown this word out, "scientific
literature" but what does that mean? So

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:08.160
scientists usually put their data in a
package and report it to the general public.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.990
And so these reports describe the latest
research that's been happening from

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:16.590
across the world, but then these
literature have to be vetted by peers.

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:21.390
And so our knowledge is shared within a
scientific community. We never want to

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:25.020
work on a project, and not share those
amazing results or those techniques that

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.060
led to those results. But, the problem is
with a lot of the scientific literature

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:33.780
they're usually published in journals,
and these journals sometimes require a

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:37.860
subscription, which is
usually expensive or cost some amount of

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:41.910
money, and if you're not affiliated with
a university it's really hard to kind of

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:48.330
get those articles. But also a lot of the
journal articles that are on there use

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:52.650
very specific jargon that makes it
difficult for not only "non-scientist" but

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:56.580
"non-specialist" in the field to digest. So
for example, my background is

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:00.539
Microbiology/Immunology, and it'll
probably be really difficult for me to

00:06:00.539 --> 00:06:05.039
read a paper that's written by-- or that
surrounds a project in you know,

00:06:05.039 --> 00:06:10.650
"Astronomy" or "Astrophysics". And so the
thing we want to highlight though that

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:14.970
in these scientific literature there's
knowledge in there that directly impact

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:19.229
our lives, it's the basis for the new
technologies that we see, the healthcare

00:06:19.229 --> 00:06:25.250
options that are given to us. But, it also
deepens our knowledge on our own biology.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:30.060
So there are two different ways that
people can access these papers, and so

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:34.710
one is what we call a "Preprint" and the
other is what we call a "Peer-Review". So,

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:39.570
what's a preprint? So, usually scientists
can come up with their work, put it in a

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:43.440
really nice way to put it on paper so that others in

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:47.760
that community can read. Now, this is a
really nice way that scientists are

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.500
showing this data in real time. And, so
they're uploaded to a specific server,

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:57.090
and people in the community, including
scientists, can actually read that paper

00:06:57.090 --> 00:07:02.340
and can share feedback directly to the
authors on the paper. So the value is

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:06.840
that you can get it in a timely manner.
You can receive the feedback from your

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:12.570
peers, but also you can-- it's a really
cool way to review what's happening in

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:18.150
that moment. And so, but, "Peer-Review" is
another way that scientists can share

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:22.140
these journal articles. And so, this is a
process in which-- these reports

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:27.390
actually go through a process where it's
vetted by experts in the field. So say

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:32.160
these scientists decided that they
wanted to put this paper in a specific

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.200
journal or organization. That organization
would recruit scientists that are experts

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:41.720
in that field. They read through the
data, they give comments back to the

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:46.200
authors, they say, "Hey, this is a great
paper, but can you please add on these

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:50.520
couple experiments?", or, "Hey, you know, I
think if you added this extra stuff

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.970
it'll be really good, but at this time
we're not going to accept your paper.". Now,

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:58.260
this is a gold standard for ensuring
quality and rigor, but this process can

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:03.030
take several months, up to years to
complete. So in a time like now, it's

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:07.470
really cool that we have this "Pre-Print",
where we can actually see the data and

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:13.890
experiments that are popping out in a
timely manner. And so this paper came

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:18.720
from one of those "Pre-Print" servers
called, "MedRxiv", and so this is just a

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.520
preprint for papers in the "Health
Sciences", but there's

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.919
also "BioRxiv" which is a server for
Biological Sciences. But, I just also want

00:08:28.919 --> 00:08:32.880
to note because it's not peer-reviewed,
they also caution that these are

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.500
preliminary reports of work that has not
been peer-reviewed, and we should not

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:41.550
rely on it for any clinical practices,
health behaviors, or should report in

00:08:41.550 --> 00:08:47.250
news media as established information. And, so this is where this paper came from,

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:51.930
"MedRxiv". So, I want to talk about "The
Styles of Scientific Reasoning". so there

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.650
are many ways to attack a question
in science.

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:59.240
And, we can approach it in many different
ways. Now, the one way that this paper

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:03.440
approaches a specific question or a goal
is through a style that we call

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:08.600
"Experimental Evaluation". And so this
means that the study involves many

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:14.000
rounds of repeating those experiments,
and documenting those results, and it's

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.220
what we call a "controlled experiment". So,
let's go back to when you were in middle

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:24.920
school or high school. Most people have
done a similar type of experiment, and so

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:28.370
for example, people who enjoy growing
plants, right? And, you have a question,

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:33.170
"Would my plant grow better in yellow
light or red light?". And so all of the

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:37.190
conditions are the same, you use the same  
plant, you water them the same, they have

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:41.300
the same nutrient. The only variable or
the thing that's "different", is a type of

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:45.410
light that is shown on this
plant. So, we have a plant under yellow

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:50.030
light, and we have a plant on the red
light. You let some time pass, the same

00:09:50.030 --> 00:09:56.450
amount of time for both ways
to assess the plant growth, and after

00:09:56.450 --> 00:10:01.040
that time you look and see what those
results were. And, so in one case you'll

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:05.000
see that, "Hey, my plant didn't grow as
much under the yellow light, but it grew

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.270
a lot under the red light, and I kept
everything else the same,

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:13.340
so I controlled my environment, so this
might hint to maybe red light has

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:17.690
something to do with plant growth".  And so again this is what we call, "controlled

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:23.510
experiments", and this style is known as
"Experimental Evaluation". So, before we get

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:28.250
started on the paper I want to do a poll.
So, I want to get everyone involved. How

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:33.100
