00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:10.439
So, now let's talk about like the main
topic at hand, coronavirus. Alright, so

00:14:10.439 --> 00:14:14.189
what is coronavirus? Like what are these
terminologies that's been used in media?

00:14:14.189 --> 00:14:19.350
And, so, COVID-19 is the terminology
that we use to describe the virus-- the

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:25.919
disease itself. So, "CO" the first two
letters in Corona, "VI" the first two

00:14:25.919 --> 00:14:32.149
letters in virus, "D" is the first letter
in disease, and the first confirmed case in

00:14:32.149 --> 00:14:38.669
2019. Now, this virus COVID-19 is caused
by a specific type of coronavirus, and

00:14:38.669 --> 00:14:42.779
we've been calling it the novel
coronavirus, but it's officially called

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:47.640
the sars-CoV-2. Now, Sars
stands for "severe acute respiratory

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:52.700
syndrome", and for those of you who
remember, in 2002, we had a previous

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:58.139
SARS outbreak. And, this particular coronavirus is actually closely related to

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:04.470
this SARS-CoV-1, so we've given the
name SARS-CoV-2 for this novel

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:10.180
coronavirus. Now, the name "coronavirus" may be new to many people but these coronaviruses--

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:13.739
coronaviruses have been around
for a very long time. If you've had the

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:19.100
common cold, chances are that might have
been caused by a human coronavirus. And,

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:23.600
so there are many different types, but
this particular one is a beta coronavirus,

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:28.319
and we named that Sars-Cov-2, or
scientists named that Sars-Cov-2.

00:15:28.319 --> 00:15:32.069
And, here's a really pretty picture I
think has been circulating many many

00:15:32.069 --> 00:15:37.769
places, and you can see that these
proteins or things on their surface kind

00:15:37.769 --> 00:15:44.279
of look like crowns. So, "Corona" is Latin
 for a crown, and so hence the name

00:15:44.279 --> 00:15:50.579
corona. But, how does this virus get into
the cell? Alright, and so how do we get

00:15:50.579 --> 00:15:55.169
sick? So, here's a image of the virus, and I
want to spend a couple seconds focusing

00:15:55.169 --> 00:15:59.429
on what this virus looks like. And, so
viruses encompasses their genetic

00:15:59.429 --> 00:16:06.119
material inside of its particle. In our
case for Sars-Cov-2 it has RNA, unlike

00:16:06.119 --> 00:16:11.369
humans where our baseline genetic
material is DNA. And, so this RNA is

00:16:11.369 --> 00:16:14.560
protected by a layer, that we call the
lipid bilayer.

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:16.880
But, in other words it's just a layer of fat.

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:19.800
And, that layer of fat has a number of

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:24.029
different proteins on the surface.
Now, these protein, this one in particular

00:16:24.029 --> 00:16:28.560
in which we call, "spike protein" is very
important for a couple things. One thing

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:32.910
is that it's important for getting into
the human cell. So, in order for this

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:38.070
virus, the coronavirus, to get into the
cell, it has to be a really good match

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:42.540
to the proteins on our human cells. And,
this particular protein in this case is

00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:47.190
called a "human ACE2 receptor". So, it's
kind of like a firm handshake. So the

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:51.060
virus is like, disguising itself
like, "Hey, you remember me let's shake

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:55.860
hands really firmly." And, once it shakes your hand it like puts itself inside of the

00:16:55.860 --> 00:17:00.360
cell. And so the virus needs to be inside
of a host cell in order to make more

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:04.050
copies of itself. And, this is why
scientists has come to a general

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:09.959
consensus of viruses not being alive,
because it can't make more viruses without

00:17:09.959 --> 00:17:14.699
the help of a host cell. So, these host
cells provide the machinery needed to

00:17:14.699 --> 00:17:19.319
make many many more of these viral
particles, which can go on to infect more

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:25.439
cells. But, this spike protein also have
another role, and it has to do with the

00:17:25.439 --> 00:17:30.419
immune response. And, so by now many of us have heard like, "Oh, do we have antibodies

00:17:30.419 --> 00:17:34.710
for the virus?", right? And, you know, "How
is our-- how do we know if someone is

00:17:34.710 --> 00:17:38.460
infected by the virus," and, "Will we be
protected?". And, so I want to briefly talk

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:42.690
about our immune response to this virus
in particular. So, your immune response has

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:47.940
two players. We have the antibodies and we have the antigens. So, what the antibodies

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:52.860
are, are these Y-shaped molecules or
proteins that are produced by our immune

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:59.460
cells. So, say our white blood cells. These
antibodies recognizes foreign particles,

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:03.630
and in our case those foreign particles
are-- would be-- coronavirus, Sars-Cov-2.

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:09.179
Now, the antibodies realize that, "Hey,
this is something I don't recognize,

00:18:09.179 --> 00:18:14.429
let's find it,
let's bind to it, let's attack it, get rid

00:18:14.429 --> 00:18:19.260
of it, but not only that, let's remember
this virus so that the next time it

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:23.760
comes around we could quickly clear the
infection without that human getting

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:28.200
sick." And, so the thing that these
antibodies bind is what we also called

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:32.320
the antigens. So it's a
dual play role. So, you have the

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:37.090
antibodies, which bind to the antigen,
which can one, get rid of that viral

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:41.500
particle, and two, it can send messages to
other immune cells in your body to

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:46.060
remember this particular antigen so the
next time it comes around we can quickly

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:52.420
get rid of it. And, so these two terms are
very important for this paper. Not only

00:18:52.420 --> 00:18:56.110
are they important, but we actually have
many different types of antibodies. Five

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:59.350
to be exact.
Five main types. But, the two I want to focus

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:06.760
on our IgM and IgG. Now, IgM is usually
the first antibody to respond to a viral

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:13.140
infection. And, IgM is usually-- swims around in a pentamer, which just means five

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:18.640
molecules put together. So, it's like a
five molecule complex. And, the second

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:23.380
antibody that comes into play is that
IgG. So, the IgM kind of like sends

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:26.950
messages to your body, and your immune
cells like, "Hey, I'm giving you this

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:30.430
information, to make sure that you can
capture this the next time it comes

00:19:30.430 --> 00:19:37.000
around." So IgG is what we call the
"secondary response" to this infection. So,

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:42.640
let's talk about the testing for COVID-19. And, so right now we have tests that

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:49.090
can tell if someone has that virus. And,
so you guys have seen where-- the videos

00:19:49.090 --> 00:19:51.940
floating around, where they take a swab
and they stick it down their nose, and

00:19:51.940 --> 00:19:57.580
they see if someone has an infection.
What we lack currently are tests to see

00:19:57.580 --> 00:20:01.630
if someone has already had the infection.
So on this scale, we have the number of

00:20:01.630 --> 00:20:06.370
days, and we have the amount of that
particular thing that's present. So,

00:20:06.370 --> 00:20:09.960
someone gets infected with that virus,

00:20:10.170 --> 00:20:16.000
and what happens is that viral load
increases, kind of hinting to the immune

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:20.440
system, "Hey, something's going wrong," and then we have this IgM antibody coming in,

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:24.970
that starts attacking this virus. But,
it's also sending messages to your other

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:29.200
immune system. And, now your IgG
antibodies come into play, which is your

00:20:29.200 --> 00:20:34.680
secondary response, and increases over
time. And, we usually call these people

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:40.120
immune to the infection, because once you
have these IgG present, as soon as you

00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:43.429
might get exposed to this virus it can
quickly attack it.

00:20:43.429 --> 00:20:47.960
But, how do we know that people are
protected from this virus or that they

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:52.190
have produced antibodies for this
infection? So, this is where this paper

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:56.269
comes in. So, one thing scientists ask is,
"What information do we have so far?".

00:20:56.269 --> 00:21:00.739
What's lacking? What do we need to work
on? So, just to know, this paper came out

00:21:00.739 --> 00:21:06.349
about a month ago, on March 18, and at
that point we have and still do have

00:21:06.349 --> 00:21:10.279
tests, or molecular assays, so it's
basically a test that looks for whether

00:21:10.279 --> 00:21:15.739
or not the virus is present. So, we
consider that a "current infection". But, we

00:21:15.739 --> 00:21:20.389
don't know when that person clears that
infection fully, and we also don't know

00:21:20.389 --> 00:21:24.499
when they've produced these antibodies
that can protect them-- protect them the

00:21:24.499 --> 00:21:29.119
next time around. And, so this paper
really focuses on like, can we develop a

00:21:29.119 --> 00:21:34.279
test to see if someone has been
previously infected with SARS-Cov-2--

00:21:34.279 --> 00:21:40.039
with SARS-Cov-2. So, what's the main
goal of this paper? So, once again, they

00:21:40.039 --> 00:21:43.909
want to see if they can develop a
version of this virus, and they're gonna

00:21:43.909 --> 00:21:48.049
use a particular protein of the virus
that can act as that antigen that I talked

00:21:48.049 --> 00:21:53.059
about, and see if we can use that antigen
to detect whether or not someone who's

00:21:53.059 --> 00:21:59.659
been infected, do have the antibodies in
their blood. So, once again, to reiterate,

00:21:59.659 --> 00:22:04.129
our bodies are really good at pumping
out these antibodies when we get sick. In

00:22:04.129 --> 00:22:09.559
our case, we're going to talk about SARS--
Sars-Cov-2. And, again these antibodies

00:22:09.559 --> 00:22:12.979
specifically recognize the proteins
on the surface, which we're going to call

00:22:12.979 --> 00:22:18.469
the antigens. So, this antibody antigen
complex, or interaction, is very important

00:22:18.469 --> 00:22:27.710
for the rest of this paper. So, again we
have this virus, and the thing when

00:22:27.710 --> 00:22:31.159
working with this virus, is that it's very
contagious. You're putting people at risk

00:22:31.159 --> 00:22:36.589
and at harm. And, so it's really hard for
scientists to take whole viruses, and try

00:22:36.589 --> 00:22:41.450
to use it to manipulate-- to come up with
tests, and things that may help with

00:22:41.450 --> 00:22:45.649
combatting this current pandemic. So,
is there a way that we can use part of

00:22:45.649 --> 00:22:50.570
this virus that are-- that can recognize
the antibodies in your immune system

00:22:50.570 --> 00:22:55.339
without causing harm to the scientists,
or when we're formulating these tests. So,

00:22:55.339 --> 00:23:00.140
what these scientists did
is that they came up with a way to make

00:23:00.140 --> 00:23:06.080
this particular protein, which is called
a spike protein, in a test tube, and use

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:10.370
it to figure out whether or not we can
use this one protein and not the virus

00:23:10.370 --> 00:23:15.350
to detect if someone do have these
antibodies. But, remember I told you these

00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:20.030
proteins, or these viruses, are really
small, antibodies are really small, so

00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:24.470
scientists have a really neat way to
actually visualize these proteins. And, we

00:23:24.470 --> 00:23:29.540
call this a "gel", right? For the lack
of a better term. And, with this gel, you

00:23:29.540 --> 00:23:34.190
see a lot of blue markings. So, these blue
markings are all proteins of different

00:23:34.190 --> 00:23:39.890
sizes that we use as a ladder or a
control. Now, scientists know by coming up

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:45.530
with the methods for this protein, the
approximate size of this protein. So, here

00:23:45.530 --> 00:23:50.090
we see the different sizes, and kDa is
"kilodaltons", and it's just a way that

00:23:50.090 --> 00:23:54.170
we measure proteins. And, so they know the approximate size of the proteins, but in

00:23:54.170 --> 00:23:58.040
this particular gel, all proteins that
they're able to produce in that test

00:23:58.040 --> 00:24:02.810
tube would light up this blue color. So,
what you can see, the scientists are

00:24:02.810 --> 00:24:07.130
confirming, that they were able to
synthetically make, in a test tube, just

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:12.740
that one piece of protein from the virus.
But, now we have the virus, which we'll

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:16.490
call the, "viral antigen", actually,
and we have this protein that's going to

00:24:16.490 --> 00:24:21.110
act as that. And, so what we want to
figure out is, "Okay, we don't have the

00:24:21.110 --> 00:24:25.760
virus anymore, we're not at risk, but can
we take that small synthetic viral

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:31.180
antigen, and use it to see if someone has
been previously infected with the Sars-Cov-2?"

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:36.040
So, just to remind everyone, when
someone gets infected with coronavirus,

00:24:36.050 --> 00:24:41.300
the antibodies bind specifically to the
antigen. In our case, that viral antigen

00:24:41.300 --> 00:24:46.370
is now that spike protein, and we want
to see if your body's antibodies to that

00:24:46.370 --> 00:24:53.600
infection can recognize just that small
part of the virus. So, they developed a

00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:58.610
method---not the method itself, but the way
that they developed it is fairly new,

00:24:58.610 --> 00:25:02.840
because they're using new molecules or
new components. But, this method has been

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:08.880
out for many years. It's called an ELISA.
An Enzyme Linked ImmunoSorbent Assay.

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:13.470
So, how many of you guys have seen what a pregnancy test looks like, alright? Or,

00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:19.500
like the HIV over-the-counter tests? They
all utilize this type of methodology to

00:25:19.500 --> 00:25:23.700
see whether or not something is present or
something is absent. Now, remember these

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:28.950
proteins-- our antibodies are really small,
so the enzyme linked is a really nice

00:25:28.950 --> 00:25:34.500
way for us to actually visualize if that
thing is present. So, in the case of this

00:25:34.500 --> 00:25:39.510
paper we've developed this viral antigen
in a test tube, right? And, we have a

00:25:39.510 --> 00:25:43.110
number of different samples from humans
who've been previously infected with

00:25:43.110 --> 00:25:47.490
coronavirus. Now, the thought process is
that in their serum, which holds their

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:52.950
antibodies, if their serum are Sars-Cov-2 specific, the scientists are

00:25:52.950 --> 00:25:57.810
hoping that it can recognize piece of
that virus. So, in the test tube, they add

00:25:57.810 --> 00:26:02.910
to serum from the human that's been
infected with coronavirus, and if they

00:26:02.910 --> 00:26:08.490
have antibodies that can bind that viral
antigen, it does so. But, how can we see

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:13.290
that it actually is binding to that
viral antigen? We have these synthetic

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:18.750
antibodies that have this really cool
link on it, that can actually allow us to

00:26:18.750 --> 00:26:23.490
visualize it in a test tube, right? So,
after we have that binding taking place,

00:26:23.490 --> 00:26:28.500
we add an activator, and so it goes from
us not being able to see anything, right?

00:26:28.500 --> 00:26:31.680
So, if there's nothing being shown on it
we're gonna assume there's no antibodies

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:37.380
present, but if something is there that's
binding to that viral antigen, you'll see

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:41.340
a color change, alright? So, basically in
this assay, what scientists are looking

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:45.420
for is whether or not this antibody and
is present, the color is there, or if that

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:50.310
antibody is absent, there's no color
there. So, what does this look like when

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:54.390
we graph it? So, what they were able to
detect is that the antibodies from

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:58.830
people who have been previously infected
were able to detect that small piece of

00:26:58.830 --> 00:27:02.850
that virus that they made in a lab, which
is the viral antigen. And, so I just want

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:08.040
to hone in on the axes. And, so on the
y-axis you have what we call the "optical

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:12.000
density", or basically the amount of light
that you can see through a machine. And,

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:16.350
so the higher that number, the more
antibodies that patient's serum have

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:20.090
that bound to that antigen. The lower the
number,

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:24.140
the less antibodies that they have
towards--- that can bind that antigen.

00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:29.020
But, we also have background signal, and
so scientists control for that amount.

00:27:29.020 --> 00:27:33.920
Now, on the bottom we want to ensure that
it is that antibody that's causing that

00:27:33.920 --> 00:27:36.950
light signal.
So, one thing scientists can do, is

00:27:36.950 --> 00:27:42.820
actually make dilutions of that antibody,
so that we can make sure that it is the

00:27:42.820 --> 00:27:46.520
antibodies that are binding to the
antigen. So, what they do is kind of like

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:50.090
having a really sweet juice, like so we
do this all the time. I'll have like the

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:53.570
Welch's Passionfruit Juice, but it's
really sweet, so if I dilute it like half

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:58.130
and half with water. So, every dilution
I'm adding more water to it, and so

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:02.360
you'll get to a point where it's super
diluted, and you don't see a signal

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:07.430
because there's very little antibody in
that test tube. Now, we're looking at the

00:28:07.430 --> 00:28:10.850
different serum samples that they're
using, and scientists did a really neat

00:28:10.850 --> 00:28:15.320
job of adding in these controls that I
talked about in the beginning. And, so we

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:21.440
have three-- we actually have four serum
samples that came from three persons who

00:28:21.440 --> 00:28:26.750
were infected with Sars-Cov-2. And, so
here we have patient one two, and three.

00:28:26.750 --> 00:28:30.890
So, one and two are the red and dark red
color, and you can see there's really

00:28:30.890 --> 00:28:37.460
high signal, leading to the thought that
these people who've been infected with

00:28:37.460 --> 00:28:41.090
SARS coronavirus do have antibodies that can recognize

00:28:41.090 --> 00:28:45.290
the antigen that these scientists made
in the lab. But, then we also have this

00:28:45.290 --> 00:28:49.490
other patient. So, we have at two
different time points, post infection. And,

00:28:49.490 --> 00:28:53.780
you can still see that they're able to
read out a signal, ensuring that the

00:28:53.780 --> 00:28:58.790
antibodies from this virus infection is
showing up and is binding to the viral

00:28:58.790 --> 00:29:03.140
antigen. Now, what are the other controls?
So, they had about 50 other patients that

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:09.080
they got serum from, in like 2019 before
the pandemic reached to U.S. And, so these

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:13.580
control samples, we can say that they've--
these control samples have actually been

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:17.360
humans who've been infected with other
viral infections. So, they've been-- either

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:21.680
been infected with the flu, they've
probably been infected with other coronaviruses

00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:25.780
that I talked about earlier. And,
so we want to ensure that this test, and

00:29:25.790 --> 00:29:30.830
this antigen, is not actually binding to
antibodies that should be specific to

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:33.920
Sars-Cov-2. Now, this one I think is
really neat.

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:38.330
So, they added a sample of someone who
had a confirmed case of another human

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:43.460
coronavirus, and that's called NL63. And,
this human coronavirus is kind of

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:47.870
special, because it actually uses that
same human receptor that Sars-Cov-2

00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:52.880
have. So, they may have some similarities
in that antigen or on that protein of--

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:58.250
on their surface. But, you can see that this
assay is really specific, because the

00:29:58.250 --> 00:30:02.630
antigen that they produced, the antibodies
that may have been in that serum, does

00:30:02.630 --> 00:30:10.550
not recognize an antigen that comes from
the Sars-Cov-2 virus. Alright, so I've

00:30:10.550 --> 00:30:14.600
shown you that the antibodies in the
serum can detect this antigen that

00:30:14.600 --> 00:30:19.820
they've made in the lab. Alright, so
just to reiterate, we have a virus that

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:25.400
infects humans, and then we have these
other data points where you see a

00:30:25.400 --> 00:30:31.610
production of IgM and IgG. What I did not
show you, was this test that I showed you

00:30:31.610 --> 00:30:35.210
previously-- or the results-- did not
indicate what type of antibodies they

00:30:35.210 --> 00:30:38.240
were. All we know that there were
antibodies in there, and they were

00:30:38.240 --> 00:30:43.040
specific enough to bind to that viral
antigen that they made in the lab. So, how

00:30:43.040 --> 00:30:47.810
can we see whether or not it's IgM or
IgG or some other antibody that's

00:30:47.810 --> 00:30:53.680
binding to this viral antigen? So, what
they did is something that we call isotyping.

00:30:53.680 --> 00:30:57.460
So, it's basically just trying to
figure out which type of antibody

00:30:57.460 --> 00:31:03.980
is actually binding to our--excuse me--
viral antigen. And, so in this slide I'm

00:31:03.980 --> 00:31:08.600
showing one data point where they test
and see the different types of

00:31:08.600 --> 00:31:12.950
antibodies that were present. And, so the
two that I wanted to highlight were IgG,

00:31:12.950 --> 00:31:17.960
which is-- so, IgG is present in four
different subtypes, and then we have the

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:23.030
IgM. Just want to zone in on the scale, so
these are ten-fold marks. So, this is like

00:31:23.030 --> 00:31:29.180
a log scale, and so the amount here is 10
times higher than the amount below. So,

00:31:29.180 --> 00:31:33.500
it shows us how much of that is present. And, the x-axis just shows us the type of

00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:38.300
antibodies. And, so all of the patients,
you can see with the different

00:31:38.300 --> 00:31:45.770
differentiations, is that they all can--
they're able to detect IgG present and IgM

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:50.660
present.
But, one thing that's been going around

00:31:50.660 --> 00:31:56.150
in the media, so if you guys watch CNN
and you listen to Cuomo, or you know any

00:31:56.150 --> 00:32:00.050
other news outlet, you hear them talk
about like, "We need to make sure that

00:32:00.050 --> 00:32:04.100
these antibody tests, when we want to
bring them out, are really good and not

00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:08.810
gonna give us any like-- you know, bad
information." So, one thing that these

00:32:08.810 --> 00:32:14.630
scientists want to make sure, is that
this test is both specific and sensitive.

00:32:14.630 --> 00:32:19.700
So, what do I mean by that? So, specificity.
And, so we want to make sure that if

00:32:19.700 --> 00:32:23.750
someone else has been infected from
another viral infection, if they were to

00:32:23.750 --> 00:32:28.280
take their test, it's not gonna come up
saying that they have antibodies to it

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:32.900
when it's not the actual virus infection
that we're looking for. So, in this case,

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:36.620
what these scientists did, remember I
told you that they also tested someone

00:32:36.620 --> 00:32:41.210
with a confirmed human coronaviruses
case that bound to the same receptor, but

00:32:41.210 --> 00:32:46.190
this test was so specific that it did
not show any light, meaning that the

00:32:46.190 --> 00:32:51.140
antibodies in this patient's serum did not
bind to the viral antigen that came from

00:32:51.140 --> 00:32:56.750
the Sars-Cov-2. Once we can ensure
that this test is specific, we can then

00:32:56.750 --> 00:33:00.860
reduce the amount of false positives. So,
we don't want the test coming up

00:33:00.860 --> 00:33:05.660
positive, when in actuality you had a
human coronavirus. You go out into the

00:33:05.660 --> 00:33:10.010
population, and thinking that you're
immune, but in actuality it was just

00:33:10.010 --> 00:33:15.200
another coronavirus that you had. Now, the
authors also try to talk about like,

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:20.870
sensitivity. So, sensitivity meaning like,
how soon can we detect if this person

00:33:20.870 --> 00:33:25.160
was able to produce antibodies to this
infection? So, if this person got infected

00:33:25.160 --> 00:33:29.720
say, two days ago, is this test sensitive
enough to pick up on maybe the little

00:33:29.720 --> 00:33:34.190
bit of IgM that's being produced? And, so
I just highlighted one of these serum

00:33:34.190 --> 00:33:38.450
samples, this person was infected about
day-- this serum was taken from this

00:33:38.450 --> 00:33:43.700
patient about day four after infection.
And, you can see that there is IgM

00:33:43.700 --> 00:33:48.380
present that they were able to detect,
and there's also IgG present. And, so it

00:33:48.380 --> 00:33:52.640
does seem to be able to pick up
antibodies that may be coming in early

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:57.500
in infection. And, so once your test is
super sensitive, you actually reduce the

00:33:57.500 --> 00:34:02.210
amount of false negatives that you have. So, you also don't want someone going out and

00:34:02.210 --> 00:34:06.380
be like, "Oh, the tests came out negative,
you know, I didn't get infected with

00:34:06.380 --> 00:34:12.800
coronavirus," and then you know, go out and is susceptible to the thing. And, you

00:34:12.800 --> 00:34:18.919
know, maybe they were already infected
with Sars-Cov-2. But, this paper, I only

00:34:18.919 --> 00:34:22.460
show you some of the main data points.
But, this paper actually did a number of

00:34:22.460 --> 00:34:26.690
different things. And, I kind of want to zone
in on the process of science. Like, this--

00:34:26.690 --> 00:34:31.700
this didn't come about in like a day or
overnight, right? It took some time, it

00:34:31.700 --> 00:34:36.590
took some trial and error. And, so these
scientists actually try to produce this

00:34:36.590 --> 00:34:41.060
viral antigen, so part of that virus, in
different types of cells, right? So,

00:34:41.060 --> 00:34:44.780
different types of cells, we can hijack
the machinery in those cells, to kind of

00:34:44.780 --> 00:34:49.669
make the protein of interest. And, I did
not show that data. These scientists also

00:34:49.669 --> 00:34:54.649
try to make different versions of this
antigen, right? So, not only could you make

00:34:54.649 --> 00:34:59.420
the full spike protein, maybe you just
make the small tip of it, and see whether or

00:34:59.420 --> 00:35:04.580
not that works. And so, I didn't show that,
but the spike protein actually worked

00:35:04.580 --> 00:35:08.570
better in their assays, or their tests,
other than the versions-- the other

00:35:08.570 --> 00:35:13.790
versions that they tried to make. I
mentioned that in this study, they only

00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:18.170
tested four samples from three patients,
so I also want to point out that this

00:35:18.170 --> 00:35:23.089
paper was just to develop the assay, and it
was not a clinical trial. So, what

00:35:23.089 --> 00:35:26.720
scientists can do, they can take this
information that was on this paper, and

00:35:26.720 --> 00:35:32.330
use it in a clinical trial, where you're
testing a larger number of humans to see

00:35:32.330 --> 00:35:37.310
if this test does actually recognize on
a broader scale. And, what I really love

00:35:37.310 --> 00:35:40.040
in this paper, is that they make this
point, and this is what I really like

00:35:40.040 --> 00:35:44.150
about MedRxiv, even though it's not
gone-- it has not gone through the

00:35:44.150 --> 00:35:48.650
peer-review process, is that we're all
working towards a similar goal. We're all

00:35:48.650 --> 00:35:53.180
really trying to get over this pandemic
as soon as possible, and go back to what

00:35:53.180 --> 00:35:57.440
used to be normal, right? And, so at the
end of the paper they made sure that

00:35:57.440 --> 00:36:02.089
they stated, "We are making all the
methods and reagents widely available to

00:36:02.089 --> 00:36:06.020
the research community in order to
support this global effort to limit and

00:36:06.020 --> 00:36:10.220
mitigate the spread of Sars-Cov-2." And,
I just know a lot of people think that,

00:36:10.220 --> 00:36:13.780
you know, we like, work in a laboratory, we
don't want anyone else to have this

00:36:13.780 --> 00:36:17.770
information, but in reality this is such a
collaborative effort, that we're pumping

00:36:17.770 --> 00:36:22.150
out this data, and we're putting out the
papers on there, so that other scientists

00:36:22.150 --> 00:36:26.260
can go into their labs, utilize the
methods that they have, and see if it can

00:36:26.260 --> 00:36:32.560
even perfect that. So, what does-- would this look like in the future? So, I spoke about

00:36:32.560 --> 00:36:37.210
their testing that are available, but
none of them are FDA approved yet. And, so

00:36:37.210 --> 00:36:41.920
I actually found there are two companies'
websites that actually already have this

00:36:41.920 --> 00:36:45.460
antibody test. Alright, so this is what
it looks like. If anyone has taken a

00:36:45.460 --> 00:36:50.590
pregnancy test before, it looks very
similar to this. And, so on the test strip,

00:36:50.590 --> 00:36:55.330
you have a control line, and so having a
control, you're ensuring that the test

00:36:55.330 --> 00:37:00.400
works. So, you're making sure that there's
something that's consistent in

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:04.450
everyone's blood or serum. So, this test
is actually a prick on your finger. So,

00:37:04.450 --> 00:37:08.140
you prick your finger, and you put the
blood in the test. And, so if this light

00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:12.190
comes up, you know that the test works.
Now, you can also have a result that's

00:37:12.190 --> 00:37:17.800
IgM positive. So, remember I said that IgM
is that antibody that responds first to

00:37:17.800 --> 00:37:22.360
this viral infection, right? And, usually if
someone is IgM positive that means that

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:26.560
they're probably still fighting off that
infection. So, this person is starting to

00:37:26.560 --> 00:37:30.850
produce those antibodies, but still have
the virus present in their bodies, so you

00:37:30.850 --> 00:37:33.850
shouldn't leave home, right? You shouldn't
leave home either way, unless you're an

00:37:33.850 --> 00:37:40.020
essential employee. But, we also have a test that could be IgG positive. And, so what IgG

00:37:40.020 --> 00:37:44.020
positive-- remember, I mentioned that
this IgG is the second one to come into

00:37:44.020 --> 00:37:47.830
play. And, usually at that point you've
already cleared the infection, and you

00:37:47.830 --> 00:37:51.550
can make the assumption that you are
immune to infection, so you're not

00:37:51.550 --> 00:37:55.150
susceptible. And, so these are the
patients that we're talking about in the

00:37:55.150 --> 00:37:58.600
news outlet, especially for healthcare
professionals. Like okay, you've recovered

00:37:58.600 --> 00:38:03.130
from this virus, you have the IgG, you're
immune. But not only that, a lot of people

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:07.480
who have a lot of IgG present, we can
actually utilize their serum to help

00:38:07.480 --> 00:38:11.200
save the lives of people who are trying
to recover from this illness. And then

00:38:11.200 --> 00:38:16.060
you can be in a phase where you're IgM
positive and IgG positive, and this just

00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:20.050
means that we're not too sure whether or
not you've cleared the virus completely

00:38:20.050 --> 00:38:24.220
because two antibodies are present. And
one thing I also want to point out is

00:38:24.220 --> 00:38:27.619
that the test-- the site also mentions
that

00:38:27.620 --> 00:38:33.560
this test is not necessarily as specific
as it should be. So, they restate that these

00:38:33.560 --> 00:38:38.140
positive results may be due to past and
present infection with another coronavirus

00:38:38.140 --> 00:38:41.180
strain that I've talked about
previously. And, the one that they

00:38:41.180 --> 00:38:45.410
mentioned, that I want to highlight, is
that NL63 which is a human coronavirus

00:38:45.410 --> 00:38:51.079
that also binds to the same human
receptor. So, this is why I think the FDA

00:38:51.079 --> 00:38:53.959
is holding out on approving some of
these tests, because you don't want

00:38:53.959 --> 00:38:58.640
people thinking that they are immune to
it, go out get the disease, and can spread

00:38:58.640 --> 00:39:04.339
that to someone else. So, moving forward.
At this point, we have the testing

00:39:04.339 --> 00:39:09.619
available to see if people have current
infection with the virus. Granted, there's

00:39:09.619 --> 00:39:14.359
all this debate and argument about like
making that accessible to everyone, but

00:39:14.359 --> 00:39:18.289
also pointing out that, remember, even if
you don't have symptoms, that doesn't

00:39:18.289 --> 00:39:21.589
mean that you're not infected with the
virus. And, I think this is why social

00:39:21.589 --> 00:39:25.579
distancing is really important with the
test that we currently have, because we

00:39:25.579 --> 00:39:30.559
have no way to test if someone has
developed immunity to this current

00:39:30.559 --> 00:39:33.769
infection. And, I think scientists are
working really hard, and it's not a

00:39:33.769 --> 00:39:37.759
matter of like, you know, if we're gonna
have that test, but I think it's like

00:39:37.759 --> 00:39:41.809
when is that test gonna be sensitive and
specific enough so that the general

00:39:41.809 --> 00:39:46.069
population can test and see whether or
not they've already recovered from that

00:39:46.069 --> 00:39:52.160
virus, and they're protected for some
amount of time. So that's all I have.

00:39:52.160 --> 00:39:59.019
Thank you, and I'd be more than happy to
take on any questions.

